For the Simulation, we decided to use AUC as the performance metric, which we clarify now in the manuscript. The ROC curve is parametrized by the lasso-penalty, which controls the number of selected variables. Therefore, by varying this parameter, we obtain variable selection specificities and sensitivities, leading to the ROC curve, and hence the AUC. This evaluation allows to compare variable selection across various model sizes.

In the manuscript: 
  "We evaluate variable selection performance by area-under-the-ROC-curve (AUC). First, we simply use the posterior inclusion probability (which links to MPM) as thresholds to obtain specificity and sensitivity. Hence, this probability parametrizes the ROC-curve. Second, for DSS, we use the Lasso penalty parameter to parametrize the ROC curve, as this parameter controls the variable selection.
